---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import PageFrame from '../components/PageFrame.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import ReadingLayout from '../components/ReadingLayout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="How 198x uses AI"
  description="Steve Hill explains how AI contributes to 198x, what he directs and reviews, and how sources, tests and execution traces help check the work."
  breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'About', href: '/about' }, { label: 'How 198x uses AI' }]}
>
  <PageFrame as="article">
    <PageHeader title="How 198x uses AI" eyebrow="By Steve Hill" />
    <ReadingLayout id="ai-reading">
      <section aria-label="Why I use AI">
        <p>I use AI throughout 198x. It writes almost all the first drafts of the code and
          lesson prose. I set the direction, develop the teaching approach, guide the
          architecture and review work through GitHub pull requests before publication.</p>

        <p>That is a substantial contribution from AI, and I want to be clear about it.
          It makes projects feasible that I would otherwise be unable to commit to
          because of the amount of work involved.</p>

        <p>I want people to be able to learn about and write code for dozens of historical
          machines. That means making the machines understandable and giving readers a
          practical way to experiment with them. A lesson needs working examples. Those
          examples need tools. The tools need to run on the computers readers actually own.</p>

        <p>I tried hard to avoid writing emulators, assemblers and build tools. Existing
          projects represented years of work, and using them was the obvious place to
          start. But I repeatedly encountered software that only ran on Windows, required
          obsolete libraries or no longer worked on modern operating systems. Websites
          had disappeared. Forks had developed different pieces of functionality,
          leaving me to work out which combination might do what I needed.</p>

        <p>Those problems become a teaching problem when every reader has to solve them
          before reaching the lesson.</p>

        <p>Building the tooling gave me a way to provide a coherent workflow across
          operating systems. It also made browser-based tools possible through WebAssembly.
          A reader can move towards writing and running code without first having to
          assemble a development environment. That serves the educational purpose of
          the project directly.</p>

        <p>The scope grew because I kept finding obstacles to what I wanted people to be
          able to do. AI made addressing those obstacles practical.</p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="my-contribution">
        <h2 id="my-contribution">What I bring to the work</h2>
        <p>I’ve been part of this scene for the better part of four decades. I grew up
          with these machines. That experience gives me a useful sense of when an
          explanation, a program or a machine’s behaviour doesn’t look right. It also
          gives me questions I want the project to answer.</p>

        <p>Experience has limits. Familiarity can help me spot a mistake, but it can also
          make a familiar misconception seem reasonable. I still need evidence.</p>

        <p>For the lessons, I’ve spent a great deal of time working through what to
          introduce, how deeply to explain it, what to leave until later and when to
          revisit a subject. I consider both what I would want to understand and what
          someone new to programming needs in order to take the next step.</p>

        <p>An explanation that helps a beginner get started may need more detail later.
          The teaching approach deliberately returns to earlier topics as readers acquire
          the understanding needed to examine them more closely. Deciding how that
          progression should work is a substantial part of my contribution.</p>

        <p>My review is less detailed in some areas than others. I’m not a particularly
          experienced Rust developer, so the Rust code underlying the emulators receives
          less detailed personal review than material closer to my expertise. I have
          guided the architecture throughout, but I would not claim to have personally
          audited every line. The validation process matters partly because my own
          knowledge cannot cover everything the project attempts.</p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="sources">
        <h2 id="sources">What the work is based on</h2>
        <p>Before developing the emulators, I assembled a substantial corpus of source
          material: documentation for the systems and their individual chips, service
          manuals, books and contemporary magazines. Other material published on the web
          generally serves as secondary evidence, unless stronger sources are unavailable.</p>

        <p>The goal has always been a high level of accuracy against the hardware, whilst
          accepting that we cannot reproduce the full physical behaviour of electrical
          circuits and voltages. The Z80 implementation models signals and timing within
          individual T-states, allowing interactions inside an instruction to be examined.
          That granularity makes detailed behaviour testable; it does not automatically
          make the implementation correct.</p>

        <p>For each feature, we attempt to validate the implementation against established
          test suites used by other emulators or against manufacturer service material.
          The lessons also draw heavily on the source corpus. Every lesson code sample
          to date has been tested using both my own emulators and assemblers and
          independently developed tools.</p>

        <p>For factual claims and citations, I use a mixture of checking material myself
          and AI-assisted cross-checking. I’m aware that AI can produce convincing errors,
          including explanations that sound much more certain than their evidence warrants.
          Citations give us something to examine. They still need to support the claim
          being made.</p>

        <p>Where documentation or tests are insufficient, we examine how existing emulators
          handle the problem. That is useful implementation evidence, with an obvious
          limitation: we may reproduce somebody else’s mistaken interpretation. Those
          uncertainties are flagged in code comments. Agreement between implementations
          is valuable, but it is not the same as agreement with the original hardware.</p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="verification">
        <h2 id="verification">What verification looks like</h2>
        <p>Asking AI to be certain is not, by itself, a verification method. It needs ways
          to investigate.</p>

        <p>The emulators include an MCP interface that allows AI tools to inspect the
          running machine: tracing execution, examining memory and watching for particular
          values or instruction addresses. I can direct an investigation and keep pressing
          for evidence while the tools provide access to what the program is actually doing.</p>

        <p>Operation Wolf’s Speedlock-7 loader provides a concrete example. Its protection
          check was failing and triggering a routine that wiped the emulated machine’s memory.</p>

        <p>Tracing execution one T-state at a time exposed the exact check that failed.
          The loader received the bit sequence <code>101000</code> where it expected{' '}
          <code>111010</code>. Examining the tape image then revealed why: our parser read
          the wrong six bits of a partially used byte.</p>

        <p>The correction was one line. Finding that line required tracing the running
          program, reconstructing the data it received and connecting that result to the
          tape parser. The fix included a regression test for the exact pulse sequence.{' '}
          <a href="https://github.com/emu198x/emu198x/commit/80ec85643752b846ecfca8801410f98a77c9cd36">The investigation and correction are recorded in Git.</a></p>

        <p>We reran the affected software. The follow-up recorded Operation Wolf progressing
          beyond the wipe and Bubble Bobble reaching game code. It also recorded that Head
          over Heels remained broken and needed a separate investigation. One successful
          correction did not justify declaring every related loader fixed.{' '}
          <a href="https://github.com/emu198x/emu198x/commit/0da6d0a96a3fcd42fc8e8850b1028c85b6f64f82">The follow-up records those different outcomes.</a></p>

        <p>Other investigations have changed earlier conclusions. Work on Z80 interrupt
          timing initially relied partly on how FUSE handled interrupts. Later, more
          detailed measurements led to a different interpretation and a correction to the
          integrated Spectrum model. The validation report explicitly supersedes the
          earlier decision and records both the evidence and the limits of the experiment.{' '}
          <a href="https://github.com/emu198x/emu198x/blob/main/test-data/z80-irq-deadline-validation.md">That report is available here.</a></p>

        <p>These examples show what the process can establish. A particular failure was
          observed, investigated and corrected. Tests exercise the corrected behaviour.
          They do not prove that the entire emulator is accurate, or that every other
          assumption is sound.</p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="preservation">
        <h2 id="preservation">Why existing information still needs work</h2>
        <p>Much of the information used by 198x already exists. The project depends on the
          work of the people who designed these systems, documented them, investigated
          them and preserved their software.</p>

        <p>Existence does not guarantee continued access. I’ve encountered that directly
          through disappearing websites and software that has become difficult to run.
          Nor does having the documents automatically give someone the understanding
          needed to use them.</p>

        <p>A chip manual, a machine’s service guide and a working program can illuminate
          different parts of the same question. Connecting them takes work. Turning that
          understanding into an implementation makes some assumptions testable. Turning
          it into a lesson helps another person acquire the understanding without having
          to repeat the entire investigation.</p>

        <p>That is part of what preservation means within 198x: keeping knowledge
          accessible, examining its connections and making it useful for learning and
          creation. It does not depend on every fact being newly discovered.</p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="responsibility">
        <h2 id="responsibility">People and responsibility</h2>
        <p>I would love to involve more of the people who built these machines and their
          software. Documentation and execution traces cannot tell us everything about
          why someone chose a particular approach, what alternatives they tried or which
          constraints mattered most at the time.</p>

        <p>Building something concrete gives those conversations a starting point.
          A reconstructed behaviour, an unanswered question or an explanation that needs
          correcting gives someone a specific opportunity to contribute. Contributions
          can be small and still matter.</p>

        <p>People discovering these machines now have something to contribute too.
          A learner can expose an assumption in a lesson that an experienced programmer
          no longer notices.</p>

        <p>Nobody needs to share my opinion of AI to contribute. Some people have ethical,
          economic or other objections to its use. Explaining my process will not resolve
          all of those disagreements, and this page is not an attempt to defend every use
          of the technology.</p>

        <p>I choose what gets published and take responsibility for it. If something is
          wrong, AI’s involvement does not excuse the error. The work needs to be open
          to examination and correction.</p>

        <p>AI has made it possible for me to pursue this project at a scale I could not
          otherwise sustain. What I want readers to gain is the ability to understand
          these machines, ask better questions about them and build something of their own.</p>
      </section>
    </ReadingLayout>
  </PageFrame>
</Layout>

<style>@layer blocks {
  section { margin-top: var(--space-10); scroll-margin-top: 6rem; }
  h2 { font-family: var(--h-font-ui); font-size: var(--heading-h4-size); line-height: var(--h-lh-h4); letter-spacing: var(--h-tr-h4); color: var(--h-ink); }
  p { max-width: 70ch; font-family: var(--h-font-ui); line-height: 1.7; color: var(--h-ink-body); margin: var(--h-space-4) 0; }
  a { color: inherit; text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: .15em; }
  a:hover { text-decoration-thickness: 2px; }
}
</style>
